<!doctype html>
<!--
  neutral-signin.html — generic sign-in landing template for phishing simulation.
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  · No brand marks, no external assets, no analytics. Simulation use only.
  · The simulation platform owns capture + redirect: the form posts back to
    this page URL (action="") and the platform intercepts the POST on submit.
  · Inputs you want in the submitted-data payload carry name="…"; drop the
    name attribute to keep a field out of the payload.
-->
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>Sign in</title>
<style>
  :root{
    color-scheme: light dark;
    --bg:#f4f5f7; --card:#ffffff; --text:#16181d; --muted:#5f6673;
    --border:#d8dbe2; --accent:#2563eb; --accent-ink:#ffffff; --danger:#b3261e;
  }
  @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark){
    :root{ --bg:#0d1017; --card:#151a23; --text:#e8eaef; --muted:#9aa2b1;
           --border:#2a3140; --accent:#5b8dff; --accent-ink:#0d1017; }
  }
  *{ box-sizing:border-box; }
  html,body{ height:100%; }
  body{
    margin:0; display:grid; place-items:center; padding:24px;
    background:var(--bg); color:var(--text);
    font:16px/1.55 system-ui,-apple-system,"Segoe UI",Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;
  }
  .card{
    width:100%; max-width:400px; background:var(--card); padding:32px 28px 28px;
    border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px;
    box-shadow:0 1px 2px rgb(0 0 0 / .06), 0 8px 24px rgb(0 0 0 / .04);
  }
  .card h1{ margin:0 0 6px; font-size:22px; font-weight:600; letter-spacing:-.01em; }
  .card .sub{ margin:0 0 24px; font-size:14px; color:var(--muted); }
  .field{ margin:0 0 16px; }
  .field label{ display:block; margin:0 0 6px; font-size:14px; font-weight:500; }
  .field input{
    width:100%; padding:10px 12px; font:inherit; color:inherit;
    background:var(--card); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px;
  }
  .field input:focus-visible{
    outline:2px solid var(--accent); outline-offset:1px; border-color:var(--accent);
  }
  .row{ display:flex; align-items:center; justify-content:space-between;
        gap:12px; margin:2px 0 20px; font-size:13.5px; }
  .row a{ color:var(--accent); text-decoration:none; }
  .row a:hover{ text-decoration:underline; }
  .check{ display:flex; align-items:center; gap:8px; color:var(--muted); }
  button{
    width:100%; padding:11px 12px; font:inherit; font-weight:600; border:0;
    border-radius:8px; cursor:pointer; color:var(--accent-ink); background:var(--accent);
  }
  button:disabled{ opacity:.65; cursor:default; }
  .status{ min-height:20px; margin:12px 0 0; font-size:13.5px; color:var(--muted); }
  .error{
    display:none; margin:0 0 16px; padding:10px 12px; font-size:14px;
    color:#b3261e; background:#b3261e14; border:1px solid #b3261e59; border-radius:8px;
  }
  .error.on{ display:block; }
  @media (max-width:420px){ .card{ padding:24px 20px; } }
</style>
</head>
<body>
  <main class="card">
    <h1>Sign in</h1>
    <p class="sub">Use your account to continue.</p>

    <!-- Optional realism — inline error banner. Toggle .on from your platform's flow.
         <p class="error" id="error" role="alert">Incorrect email or password. Please try again.</p>
    -->

    <form id="signin" action="" method="POST">
      <!--
        action="" posts back to this page URL, where the simulation platform
        intercepts the request (documented GoPhish pattern: POST with no action).
        Some platforms instead expect an explicit endpoint or a hook attribute
        (e.g. data-phishing-form) — check your landing-page docs.

        rid: the per-recipient token rides in the page URL and is mapped to
        recipient + campaign when the POST lands. If your platform prefers it
        as a field:  <input type="hidden" name="rid" value="{{.RId}}">
      -->

      <div class="field">
        <label for="email">Email address</label>
        <input id="email" name="email" type="email" inputmode="email"
               autocomplete="username" autocapitalize="none" spellcheck="false"
               placeholder="name@example.com" required>
      </div>

      <div class="field">
        <label for="password">Password</label>
        <input id="password" name="password" type="password"
               autocomplete="current-password" required>
        <!-- Field-name governance: every named input appears in the submitted-data
             payload under that name (key → list of values). Rename or drop the
             name to change what is captured; keep name="password" only when
             password capture is enabled and policy allows storing it. -->
      </div>

      <div class="row">
        <label class="check"><input type="checkbox" name="remember" value="yes"> Keep me signed in</label>
        <a href="#">Forgot password?</a>
      </div>

      <button id="submit" type="submit">Sign in</button>
      <p class="status" id="status" role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
    </form>
    <!-- Post-submit behavior — education/reveal page, redirect to the real site,
         or both — is configured on the campaign/landing-page object, not here. -->
  </main>

  <script>
    // Minimal enhancement only: lock the button on submit so the click feels
    // final. The platform handles everything after the POST. Keep scripts tiny,
    // and never put an onclick redirect on the submit button — it overrides
    // submission and kills the submitted-data event.
    document.getElementById('signin').addEventListener('submit', function () {
      var b = document.getElementById('submit');
      b.disabled = true;
      b.textContent = 'Signing in\u2026';
      document.getElementById('status').textContent = 'Verifying your account\u2026';
    });
  </script>
</body>
</html>
```

WIRE-UP NOTES
- Form contract: action="" + method="POST" so the POST lands back on the sim server for interception — the documented GoPhish pattern, and the known "secret recipe" for post-submit redirects.[1][4] If the platform rewrites or strips the action attribute, that's platform behavior, not breakage — redirect targets live on the landing-page object.[3]
- name = capture contract: every named input appears in the Submitted Data payload as key → list of values.[9] With capture options unchecked, GoPhish strips name attributes on save, so nothing submits.[1] Naming conventions matter: recognized username/password names (username, email, loginfmt / password, passwd, …) get stored as clean credential pairs on some platforms; unfamiliar names fall back to a raw name=value dump.[5]
- Password governance: keep name="password" only when password capture is enabled and policy allows. Microsoft's own custom-page guidance is to omit the name attribute and reference fields via type/id/class so values aren't sent as plaintext.[6] Capture defaults (passwords off) are platform-side settings.[2]
- Password managers: autocomplete="off" is widely ignored; managers can fill hidden fields and submit REAL passwords — the classic case was Microsoft's simulator's two-step flow, where a manager filled a hidden password field; the suggested mitigation was autocomplete="new-password". Template ships realistic autocomplete attributes; switch deliberately per program policy.[7]
- Button gotchas: keep type="submit" and no onclick redirect — onclick overrides submission and you lose the Submitted Data event.[8]
- rid: rides in the page URL; the platform maps it to recipient + campaign on POST.[2] Optional hidden-field variant sits in the comments ({{.RId}}).
- Styling rationale: system font stack, one accent, no CDN/no images/no analytics — neutral baseline you can skin per scenario later; responsive, dark-mode aware, and a11y-clean (labels, focus-visible rings, aria-live status). Neutral also means "convincing canvas" without impersonating anything real.
- Test as a single-recipient campaign to yourself before a real one; post-submit reveal/redirect is configured platform-side.

Sources:
[1] https://docs.getgophish.com/user-guide/faq — Gophish FAQ - minimal capture form (action='' POST, name attrs)
[2] https://docs.getgophish.com/user-guide/documentation/landing-pages — Gophish - Landing pages (capture flags, redirect, rid)
[3] https://github.com/gophish/gophish/issues/3338 — Gophish issue - action='' stripping; redirect configured platform-side
[4] https://github.com/gophish/gophish/issues/2092 — Gophish issue - redirect recipe: POST with no action
[5] https://docs.openaev.io/latest/usage/build/components/phishing/landing-pages — OpenAEV - landing pages (data-phishing-form, recognized field names)
[6] https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/defender-office-365/attack-simulation-training-login-pages — MS AST - login pages (field naming, password advice, two-page)
[7] https://adminotes.blogspot.com/2019/08/ms-spear-phishing-attack-simulator-can.html — Password managers can fill sim pages; autocomplete=off unreliable
[8] https://github.com/gophish/gophish/issues/1765 — Gophish issue - submit button must be type=submit; no onclick override
[9] https://docs.getgophish.com/user-guide/documentation/webhooks — Gophish - webhooks (named fields become payload key/values)

⚙️ Client submit handler — serialise → POST → respond. Wired into the template and verified end-to-end in a real browser.

Updated file: /home/ubuntu/sim_templates/neutral-signin.html (two changes vs. the previous version: `data-inline-target` added to `<main>`, and the old two-line script replaced with the handler below).

```html
<script>
  /*
    Client submit handler — serialise → POST → handle the response.
    ---------------------------------------------------------------
    · Transport: POST back to the same page URL (action="" semantics) so the
      rid stays in the query string and the platform intercepts the request.
      Set CONFIG.endpoint to post elsewhere (cross-origin needs CORS).
    · Serialisation mirrors a native form POST: every control with a name,
      not disabled; unchecked checkboxes/radios are skipped.
    · Response handling: a platform redirect (302 chain) is followed in the
      address bar; a 200 renders into [data-inline-target] when onSuccess is
      'inline' (education/reveal page) — otherwise the page just settles.
      HTTP errors keep the form usable; network failures fall back to ONE
      native form POST so the submission still reaches the platform.
    · Don't add this handler if your platform installs its own submit
      interception (e.g. data-phishing-form) — you'd double-post.
    · Keep the submit button a real type="submit" with no onclick redirect.
  */
  (function () {
    'use strict';

    var CONFIG = {
      endpoint: '',                 // '' = current URL (same-origin, keeps rid)
      onSuccess: 'inline',          // 'inline' (render response) | 'redirect' (go to fallbackUrl)
      inlineTarget: '[data-inline-target]',
      fallbackUrl: '',              // used when onSuccess === 'redirect' ('' = stay put)
      timeoutMs: 10000,
      nativeFallback: true,         // network failure ⇒ one native form POST
      sendingText: 'Signing in\u2026',
      checkingText: 'Verifying your account\u2026',
      successText: 'You\u2019re signed in.',
      errorText: 'Something went wrong. Please try again.'
    };

    var form = document.getElementById('signin');
    if (!form) return;

    var button = document.getElementById('submit');
    var statusEl = document.getElementById('status');
    var originalLabel = button ? button.textContent : '';
    var busy = false;
    var triedNative = false;

    function setStatus(text) { if (statusEl) statusEl.textContent = text || ''; }

    function restoreButton() {
      if (button) { button.disabled = false; button.textContent = originalLabel; }
      form.removeAttribute('aria-busy');
    }

    function serialise(formEl) {
      var pairs = [];
      var els = formEl.elements;
      for (var i = 0; i < els.length; i++) {
        var el = els[i];
        if (!el.name || el.disabled) continue;
        var type = (el.type || '').toLowerCase();
        if (type === 'submit' || type === 'button' || type === 'reset' || type === 'file') continue;
        if ((type === 'checkbox' || type === 'radio') && !el.checked) continue;
        pairs.push(encodeURIComponent(el.name) + '=' + encodeURIComponent(el.value));
      }
      return pairs.join('&');
    }

    form.addEventListener('submit', function (event) {
      event.preventDefault();          // we own the transport now
      if (busy) return;
      busy = true;

      if (button) { button.disabled = true; button.textContent = CONFIG.sendingText; }
      form.setAttribute('aria-busy', 'true');
      setStatus(CONFIG.checkingText);

      var url = CONFIG.endpoint || window.location.href;
      var controller = ('AbortController' in window) ? new AbortController() : null;
      var timer = setTimeout(function () { if (controller) controller.abort(); }, CONFIG.timeoutMs);

      fetch(url, {
        method: 'POST',
        credentials: 'same-origin',
        redirect: 'follow',            // platform redirects execute (default, stated explicitly)
        headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/x-www-form-urlencoded;charset=UTF-8' },
        body: serialise(form),
        signal: controller ? controller.signal : undefined
      }).then(function (res) {
        clearTimeout(timer);

        // 1) Redirect chain followed server-side: mirror it in the address bar.
        if (res.redirected && res.url) { window.location.assign(res.url); return; }

        // 2) No redirect: non-2xx is a soft failure the user can retry.
        if (!res.ok) { var e = new Error('HTTP ' + res.status); e.httpStatus = res.status; throw e; }

        return res.text().then(function (html) {
          var target = document.querySelector(CONFIG.inlineTarget);
          if (CONFIG.onSuccess === 'inline' && target && html && html.trim()) {
            target.innerHTML = html;   // platform-owned HTML: education / reveal page
          } else if (CONFIG.onSuccess === 'redirect' && CONFIG.fallbackUrl) {
            window.location.assign(CONFIG.fallbackUrl);
            return;
          } else {
            setStatus(CONFIG.successText);  // event recorded; nothing to render
          }
          busy = false;
        });
      }).catch(function (err) {
        clearTimeout(timer);

        var serverAnswered = err && err.httpStatus;
        if (CONFIG.nativeFallback && !serverAnswered && !triedNative) {
          // Network-level failure (offline / blocked / timeout): hand the
          // submission to the browser as ONE native form POST. form.submit()
          // bypasses this listener, so there is no recursion. Duplicate rows
          // are possible if the original request actually landed — acceptable
          // for simulation capture; tune away if you disagree.
          triedNative = true;
          setStatus(CONFIG.checkingText);
          form.submit();
          return;
        }

        busy = false;
        restoreButton();
        setStatus(CONFIG.errorText);
      });
    });
  })();
  </script>
```

✅ VERIFIED END-TO-END (mock platform + real browser)
- Rig: local mock endpoint (/tmp/simtest/server.py) logs every POST and has two modes; test copies auto-fill (email user@example.com, canary password, remember checked) and auto-submit. Browser: CloakBrowser/Playwright — note this host's plain headless Chrome hangs on all http(s) requests (file:// only), so the cloak stack carried the test.
- Inline run (200 + education HTML): captured body `email=user%40example.com&password=canary-PW-123&remember=yes` — exactly native form encoding; page then showed "That was a simulation." + the echoed submission → swap into [data-inline-target] executed.
- Redirect run (302 → /done.html): same captured body; browser ended on http://127.0.0.1:8893/done.html with DONE-MARKER → the res.redirected mirror worked.
- Console: clean apart from the mock rig's favicon 404. Raw captures: /tmp/simtest/events.jsonl (replay: `python3 /tmp/simtest/server.py`, then open harness.html?rid=TEST).

WIRE-UP NOTES
- Transport is deliberate: the handler posts to the current URL (action="" semantics), so rid stays in the query and the platform intercepts the request — the documented GoPhish pattern, and the payload matches the platform's field-naming capture rule.[1][2]
- If your platform installs its own submit interception (OpenAEV-style `data-phishing-form`), drop this handler — one owner per submit, or you double-post.[3]
- Redirects: fetch follows them by default[6]; the handler mirrors the final URL from `res.redirected`/`res.url` into the address bar[5]. (MDN's caveat — `redirected` can't *prevent* a redirect, only report one — doesn't matter here; this is mirroring, not a security decision.)[5]
- Keep the submit button `type="submit"` with no onclick redirect — onclick overrides submission and the captured event is lost.[4]
- Failure semantics: HTTP error → form re-enables for retry; network/timeout → ONE native form POST fallback (no recursion; duplicates possible if the first request actually landed).
- Custom endpoint: cross-origin needs CORS on the platform side; the same-origin default avoids it. Swap behavior is one CONFIG line: `onSuccess: 'redirect'` + `fallbackUrl` to just send users on after submit.

Sources:
[1] https://docs.getgophish.com/user-guide/faq — Gophish FAQ - form action='' POST + name attrs
[2] https://docs.getgophish.com/user-guide/documentation/webhooks — Gophish webhooks - submitted-data payload shape
[3] https://docs.openaev.io/latest/usage/build/components/phishing/landing-pages — OpenAEV - platform-side interception (data-phishing-form)
[4] https://github.com/gophish/gophish/issues/1765 — Gophish issue - submit button type=submit, no onclick override
[5] https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Response/redirected — MDN - Response.redirected (final url after redirects)
[6] https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Request/redirect — MDN - Request.redirect (default follow)
